# Const Maps Over TypeScript Enums

TypeScript `enum` produces brittle runtime objects and confuses tree-shaking.
Use a `const` object + a derived union type instead.

## Pattern

```ts
// ✅ Const map — new code must follow this pattern
export const ClientGroup = {
  CUSTOMER: 'CUSTOMER',
  VENDOR: 'VENDOR',
  AGENT: 'AGENT',
  OTHER_CLIENT: 'OTHER_CLIENT',
} as const

export type ClientGroup = (typeof ClientGroup)[keyof typeof ClientGroup]
```

## Migration from existing enums in `src/types/enum.ts`

The existing enums (`CLIENT_GROUP`, `DEPARTMENT`, `JOB_TITLE`, `STATUS`) are
legacy. When you **touch** a file that imports one of them, convert only the
enum values you use in that file to the const-map form above. Do not attempt
a project-wide rename in one commit.

### Zod schemas

```ts
// ✅ z.enum() accepts a const tuple — derive it from the map
import { ClientGroup } from '@/types/client-group'

clientGroup: z.enum(
  Object.values(ClientGroup) as [ClientGroup, ...ClientGroup[]]
)
```

### RTK Query / API payloads

```ts
// Pass the value directly — no enum member lookup needed
body: { clientGroup: ClientGroup.CUSTOMER }
```

### Comparisons

```ts
// ✅
if (row.clientGroup === ClientGroup.CUSTOMER) { … }

// ❌ — never use numeric or magic-string comparisons
if (row.clientGroup === 'CUSTOMER') { … }
```

## Rules

- **Never** add a new `enum` declaration anywhere in the project.
- The type alias must share the **same name** as the const object so call
  sites need only one import.
- Export const maps from a dedicated file per domain
  (e.g. `src/types/client-group.ts`), not from a shared `enum.ts` barrel.
